{"name":"El Caramelo","hex":"#946e48","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"caramelo","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A mixed-breed dog originating in Brazil, known for its caramel-colored coat."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/caramelo"}],"entries":[{"title":"Caramelo","blocks":[{"text":"Caramelo is a 2002 epic novel spanning a hundred years of Mexican history by American author Sandra Cisneros. It was inspired by her Mexican heritage and childhood in the barrio of Chicago, Illinois. The main character, Lala, is the only girl in a family of seven children and her family often travels between Chicago and Mexico City. Because Cisneros also has six brothers and her family moved frequently when she was a child, the novel is semi-autobiographical. The novel could also be called a Bildungsroman, as it focuses on Lala's development from childhood onward. It was shortlisted for the 2004 International Dublin Literary Award. It was simultaneously released in English and Spanish, with the Spanish translation done by Liliana Valenzuela."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/b/b5/Caramelo_novel.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Caramelo"}],"images":[],"sounds":{},"pictures":[{"title":"Vieira salteada al limón, caramelo de su coral y tomate confitado al tomillo (4181473805)","image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/65/Vieira_salteada_al_lim%C3%B3n%2C_caramelo_de_su_coral_y_tomate_confitado_al_tomillo_%284181473805%29.jpg/500px-Vieira_salteada_al_lim%C3%B3n%2C_caramelo_de_su_coral_y_tomate_confitado_al_tomillo_%284181473805%29.jpg","ratio":1.3333333333333333,"link":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vieira_salteada_al_lim%C3%B3n,_caramelo_de_su_coral_y_tomate_confitado_al_tomillo_(4181473805).jpg","share":0.32125},{"title":"Sèvres - Magot - Pajou, buste de Madame du Barry 08","image":"https://thumb.wiki